  "comment": {
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "5bba58f3eb6f6e2ccfd097c6",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png",
       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Ben Pennell",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=5bba58f3eb6f6e2ccfd097c6",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": {
      "content": [
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "After some testing, I was able to reproduce the issue, but also was able to avoid the problem without any code changes. ",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "I had a fedora 5 instance running at localhost:8043, did the export as follows:",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "java -jar fcrepo-import-export-1.1.0.jar \\\n  --dir fcrepo-5-export \\\n  --user fedora_admin:fedora_admin \\\n  --mode export \\\n  --repositoryRoot http://localhost:8043/fcrepo/rest \\\n  --resource http://localhost:8043/fcrepo/rest \\\n  --binaries \\\n  --versions",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "codeBlock"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "Then ran the upgrade util as follows:",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "java -jar fcrepo-upgrade-utils-6.2.0.jar \\\n  --input-dir fcrepo-5-export \\\n  --output-dir fcrepo-6-home \\\n  --source-version 5+ \\\n  --target-version 6+ \\\n  --base-uri http://localhost:8043/fcrepo/rest",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "codeBlock"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "and then inspected the generated OCFL. The binary descriptions with extra triples had the correct “info:fedora/” prefix in subject URIs. I then brought fedora 6 up on port 8880 so that the port would not match the original server with:",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "java -Dfcrepo.home=fcrepo-6-home -jar fcrepo-webapp-6.4.1-jetty-console.jar",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "codeBlock"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "Inspecting the binary descriptions in the UI, the properties belong to the binary rather than to a separate resource like originally observed.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "If I do the same steps, but when running the upgrade util provide a different base-uri that doesn’t match the fedora 5 server (for example, “",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "type": "code"
           }
          ],
          "text": "http://localhost:8880/fcrepo/rest",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": "\"), then I get the issue observed in this ticket: triples in the binary description rdf in the OCFL will have subjects with the old “",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "attrs": {
             "href": "http://localhost:8043/”"
            },
            "type": "link"
           }
          ],
          "text": "http://localhost:8043/”",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": " prefix, and when viewed in the web interface the properties will appear in the Other Resources section. ",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "This behavior makes sense however, since there is not actually an import step. The upgrade util transforms the fedora 5 export into OCFL format, and while doing so it also transforms the URIs in the RDF, replacing all subject and object URIs that contain the ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "type": "code"
           }
          ],
          "text": "base-uri",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": " with the host agnostic “info:fedora/”. And then fedora 6 is started with the generated OCFL as its data directory, with the transformed URIs in the RDF if the correct fedora 5 base-uri was provided.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "So as long as the base-uri matches the URI in the fedora 5 export, the upgrade util appears to work as expected when migrating to a new host since the generated OCFL contains URIs that use the generic “info:fedora/” prefix.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       }
      ],
      "type": "doc",
      "version": 1
     },
