     "body": "<p>After some testing, I was able to reproduce the issue, but also was able to avoid the problem without any code changes. </p>\n\n<p>I had a fedora 5 instance running at localhost:8043, did the export as follows:</p>\n\n<div class=\"preformatted panel\" style=\"border-width: 1px;\"><div class=\"preformattedContent panelContent\">\n<pre>java -jar fcrepo-import-export-1.1.0.jar \\\n  --dir fcrepo-5-export \\\n  --user fedora_admin:fedora_admin \\\n  --mode export \\\n  --repositoryRoot http://localhost:8043/fcrepo/rest \\\n  --resource http://localhost:8043/fcrepo/rest \\\n  --binaries \\\n  --versions</pre>\n</div></div>\n\n<p>Then ran the upgrade util as follows:</p>\n\n<div class=\"preformatted panel\" style=\"border-width: 1px;\"><div class=\"preformattedContent panelContent\">\n<pre>java -jar fcrepo-upgrade-utils-6.2.0.jar \\\n  --input-dir fcrepo-5-export \\\n  --output-dir fcrepo-6-home \\\n  --source-version 5+ \\\n  --target-version 6+ \\\n  --base-uri http://localhost:8043/fcrepo/rest</pre>\n</div></div>\n\n<p>and then inspected the generated OCFL. The binary descriptions with extra triples had the correct “info:fedora/” prefix in subject URIs. I then brought fedora 6 up on port 8880 so that the port would not match the original server with:</p>\n\n<div class=\"preformatted panel\" style=\"border-width: 1px;\"><div class=\"preformattedContent panelContent\">\n<pre>java -Dfcrepo.home=fcrepo-6-home -jar fcrepo-webapp-6.4.1-jetty-console.jar</pre>\n</div></div>\n\n<p>Inspecting the binary descriptions in the UI, the properties belong to the binary rather than to a separate resource like originally observed.</p>\n\n<p>If I do the same steps, but when running the upgrade util provide a different base-uri that doesn’t match the fedora 5 server (for example, “<tt><a href=\"http://localhost:8880/fcrepo/rest\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8880/fcrepo/rest</a></tt>\"), then I get the issue observed in this ticket: triples in the binary description rdf in the OCFL will have subjects with the old “<a href=\"http://localhost:8043/”\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8043/”</a> prefix, and when viewed in the web interface the properties will appear in the Other Resources section. </p>\n\n<p>This behavior makes sense however, since there is not actually an import step. The upgrade util transforms the fedora 5 export into OCFL format, and while doing so it also transforms the URIs in the RDF, replacing all subject and object URIs that contain the <tt>base-uri</tt> with the host agnostic “info:fedora/”. And then fedora 6 is started with the generated OCFL as its data directory, with the transformed URIs in the RDF if the correct fedora 5 base-uri was provided.</p>\n\n<p>So as long as the base-uri matches the URI in the fedora 5 export, the upgrade util appears to work as expected when migrating to a new host since the generated OCFL contains URIs that use the generic “info:fedora/” prefix.</p>",
